From f75bed384f9111c9ce985668eceb81a3746ef69f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: energie-teilen <noreply@energie-teilen.de>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 18:17:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(discovery): a route per tool, a documented API, and a
 real sitemap
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every engine in this repository was reachable only by scrolling one long
page. A section inside a page cannot be indexed, cannot be linked to from
an answer, and cannot rank for the question it answers — so the metering
generator, the allocation comparison and the market-communication layer
were, commercially, invisible. Nine API endpoints had no documentation at
all, which meant nobody outside this repository could evaluate them.

shared/routes.ts is now the single manifest every part reads: the router,
the per-route document head, the sitemap, the header, the footer and the
tests that check none of them has drifted. Each route records the question
it answers, because a page without one has no reason to rank and no reason
to exist.

Four new routes:
  /messkonzept            which metering concept, how many meters, which
                          roles, and the critical path to operation
  /aufteilungsschluessel  three allocation keys compared on one dataset,
                          with the difference in self-consumption computed
  /marktkommunikation     the 92 / 96 / 100 quarter-hour grid and the
                          generated MSCONS message
  /api                    all nine endpoints, each with a runnable curl
                          example, a real response, and what it refuses

Each is a real document, not a client-side title swap. scripts/build-static
emits one HTML file per route with that route's title, description,
canonical URL and JSON-LD, so a crawler, a link preview and a chat unfurl
read the right answer without executing anything; React hydrates over it
unchanged. The server serves those documents at the path that is canonical
rather than redirecting to a trailing slash.

The sitemap is generated from the manifest, so it can no longer list a page
that does not exist or omit one that does. It went from four entries —
three of them legal pages — to nine.

Also fixed along the way, each found by the browser suite:
  - the header overflowed once the tools were added, hiding the last link
    under the call to action
  - "Rechner" appeared twice in navigation, as an anchor and as a route
  - the footer advertised two tools as "in Vorbereitung" after they had
    been built, and now reads from the manifest instead
  - the API reference was absent from navigation because it is a reference
    rather than a tool
  - three contrast failures between 3.45:1 and 4.41:1
  - parameter tables on the API page could not be scrolled by keyboard

530 unit tests and 357 browser checks pass. Typecheck and build clean.
